var e=`Teaching anime facts for Pulse. Visuals come from tags in shot.ts. Never Dex-Pikachu-as-the-show.`,t=`2026-09-16`,n=204,r=[{id:`ani-jp-1997`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`The anime premiered in Japan on April 1, 1997`,stat:`1997`,sub:`Pocket Monsters on TV Tokyo. The games were already a year old.`,why:`Japan aired the first episode on April 1, 1997, more than a year after Red and Green launched on February 27, 1996. The West did not get the cartoon that spring. US syndication started in 1998, so a 1997 Japan date is not a 1997 English air date.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`indigo`,`ash`]},{id:`ani-us-1998`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`The United States first saw the show in 1998`,stat:`1998`,sub:`September 8 syndication. Japan had already run a year.`,why:`The English run began September 8, 1998. Some markets sneaked Battle Aboard the St. Anne first, then ran Pokémon - I Choose You! as the real start. That is a 1998 US window, not the April 1, 1997 Japan premiere.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`indigo`,`ash`,`4kids`]},{id:`ani-4kids`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`The 4Kids dub is why a generation says Pokémon`,stat:`4KIDS`,sub:`They changed names, music, and scenes for US TV.`,why:`4Kids Entertainment dubbed the show for US kids' TV from 1998, turning Satoshi, Takeshi, and Kasumi into Ash, Brock, and Misty. Japan never saw that cut, soundtrack, or those name swaps. Later English dubs, after Pokémon USA took the work in-house, moved closer to the Japanese show.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`4kids`,`indigo`,`ash`]},{id:`ani-ikue`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Ikue Ōtani has voiced Pikachu since 1997`,stat:`1997`,sub:`Same Japanese voice from episode one through Horizons.`,why:`Ikue Ōtani has been Pikachu since the April 1, 1997 premiere, and most dubs keep those cries instead of translating them. She also voices Captain Pikachu in Horizons. The games use a different cry, and the cards are silent.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`indigo`,`captain`]},{id:`ani-ash-satoshi`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`In Japan, Ash is named Satoshi`,stat:`JP`,sub:`The English show renamed him. Same boy, same hat.`,why:`Japan called the lead Satoshi from day one, after creator Satoshi Tajiri. The English dub picked Ash Ketchum. Horizons in 2023 started a new lead, so Satoshi's run is the old show.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`ash`,`indigo`]},{id:`ani-ep001`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Episode one is Pokémon, I Choose You`,stat:`EP001`,sub:`Japan April 1, 1997. US September 8, 1998.`,why:`EP001 is the Japan start: a late Pallet Town kid, a stubborn Pikachu, a Spearow flock. Japan aired it April 1, 1997. The US aired it September 8, 1998 after a sneak of a later episode.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`indigo`,`ash`]},{id:`ani-games-first`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`The games launched before the cartoon`,stat:`1996`,sub:`Red and Green, Japan, February 27. TV came next.`,why:`Pokémon Red and Green hit Japan on February 27, 1996, and the anime followed on April 1, 1997. In the US the cartoon often arrived first in a kid's life, in 1998, while Red and Blue landed later that year. Japan saw Game Boy, then TV; the West often saw TV, then Game Boy.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`indigo`]},{id:`ani-theme-us`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`The English Pokémon Theme is a 4Kids song`,stat:`THEME`,sub:`Jason Paige. Japan used a different opening.`,why:`Gotta catch 'em all is the 4Kids English opening, sung by Jason Paige. Japan opened with Rica Matsumoto as Satoshi on Aim to Be a Pokémon Master. The Japanese openings never used that 4Kids track.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`4kids`,`indigo`]},{id:`ani-rica`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Rica Matsumoto voiced Satoshi for the whole Ash run`,stat:`JP`,sub:`1997 to 2023 in Japan. English used two Ash voices.`,why:`Rica Matsumoto was Satoshi from the 1997 premiere through the Journeys finale in 2023. English split the role: Veronica Taylor through Battle Frontier, then Sarah Natochenny. Japan did not recast the lead when the US dub changed studios.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`ash`,`indigo`]},{id:`ani-veronica`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Veronica Taylor was the first English Ash`,stat:`1998`,sub:`4Kids. She also voiced May in Advanced.`,why:`Veronica Taylor voiced Ash from the 1998 US start through most of Advanced. When Pokémon USA moved the dub in-house for Battle Frontier, Sarah Natochenny took Ash. Taylor's Ash is the 4Kids seasons.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`ash`,`4kids`,`indigo`]},{id:`ani-natochenny`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Sarah Natochenny voiced Ash from Battle Frontier on`,stat:`AG146`,sub:`2006 English. Japan never changed Satoshi's voice.`,why:`Sarah Natochenny became English Ash starting with Battle Frontier, when the dub left 4Kids for TAJ Productions. That is an English studio change, not a Japan recast. She kept Ash through the 2023 Journeys ending, then took smaller Horizons roles.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`ash`]},{id:`ani-oak`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Professor Oak hands out the Kanto starters in Pallet Town`,stat:`OAK`,sub:`Yukinari Okido in Japan. Ash was late and got Pikachu.`,why:`Professor Oak lives in Pallet Town and gives new trainers Bulbasaur, Charmander, or Squirtle. Ash overslept and received Pikachu instead. Japan names him Yukinari Okido; he is the Kanto professor, not the Horizons professor.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`ash`,`indigo`]},{id:`ani-brock`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Brock left Pewter Gym to travel with Ash`,stat:`BROCK`,sub:`Takeshi in Japan. He cooks. He is not the lead.`,why:`Brock is the Pewter City Gym Leader who joins Ash after the first gym battle. Japan calls him Takeshi. He stays through Johto, returns in Advanced and Diamond and Pearl, then leaves for Pokémon doctor work.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`ash`,`indigo`]},{id:`ani-misty`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Misty is the Cerulean Gym Leader who bikes with Ash`,stat:`MISTY`,sub:`Kasumi in Japan. She leaves after Johto.`,why:`Misty chases Ash after he wrecks her bike in episode one, then stays through Indigo, Orange, and Johto. Japan calls her Kasumi. After Johto she returns to Cerulean Gym, and Advanced does not keep her in the traveling party.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`ash`,`indigo`]},{id:`ani-rocket`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Team Rocket is Jessie, James, and talking Meowth`,stat:`TR`,sub:`Musashi, Kojiro, and Nyarth in Japan.`,why:`Jessie, James, and Meowth chase Pikachu across the Ash years. Japan names them Musashi, Kojiro, and Nyarth. Horizons replaced that trio as the weekly villains with the Explorers.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`ash`,`indigo`]},{id:`ani-gary`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Gary Oak is Ash's first rival, not a gym leader`,stat:`GARY`,sub:`Shigeru in Japan. Grandson of Professor Oak.`,why:`Gary starts the same day as Ash, with a proper starter, and spends Indigo as the smug rival. Japan calls him Shigeru. He later becomes a researcher, and he is original-series rivalry, not Paul or Leon.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`ash`,`indigo`]},{id:`ani-delia`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Ash's mother Delia stays in Pallet Town`,stat:`DELIA`,sub:`Hanako in Japan. Mr. Mime helps at home.`,why:`Delia Ketchum waves Ash off from Pallet Town and appears when he comes home. Japan names her Hanako. A Mr. Mime, Mimey in English, helps around the house.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`ash`,`indigo`]},{id:`ani-no-ball`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Ash's Pikachu refuses to stay in a Poké Ball`,stat:`OS`,sub:`That is a show choice. Game Pikachu go in balls.`,why:`From episode one, Ash's Pikachu walks beside him instead of riding in a ball. That is an anime character beat, not a game rule. Captain Pikachu in Horizons also stays out, but he belongs to Friede.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`ash`,`indigo`,`captain`]},{id:`ani-meowth-talks`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Team Rocket's Meowth learned to talk`,stat:`MEOWTH`,sub:`A show gag. Wild Meowth in the games do not chat.`,why:`This Meowth taught himself human speech to impress a female Meowth, then joined Jessie and James. Inuko Inuyama has voiced him in Japanese since 1997. Talking Meowth is an anime invention, not a Pokédex entry.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`indigo`]},{id:`ani-jp-names`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Japan never called them Ash, Brock, and Misty`,stat:`JP`,sub:`Satoshi, Takeshi, Kasumi. The dub renamed the kids.`,why:`The Japanese show uses Satoshi, Takeshi, and Kasumi. English 4Kids mapped those to Ash, Brock, and Misty to match the games' English names. If you watch a Japanese credit, you will not see Ash.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`ash`,`indigo`,`4kids`]},{id:`ani-tracey`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Tracey Sketchit replaced Brock in the Orange Islands`,stat:`S2`,sub:`Kenji in Japan. A watcher, not a gym leader.`,why:`When Brock left for a stretch, Tracey joined Ash and Misty through the Orange Islands. Japan calls him Kenji. Brock returns for Johto, so Tracey is an Orange Islands companion, not a Kanto gym leader.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`ash`,`indigo`]},{id:`ani-indigo`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Indigo League is the English name for season one`,stat:`S1`,sub:`Japan just called it Pocket Monsters. Kanto.`,why:`The West split the original Japanese run into seasons. Season 1 is Indigo League: Kanto gyms, then the Indigo Plateau. Japan did not use that season title on air.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`indigo`,`ash`]},{id:`ani-indigo-loss`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Ash lost the Indigo League in the round of sixteen`,stat:`EP080`,sub:`Ritchie. Eight badges did not mean a trophy.`,why:`Ash collected eight Kanto badges and still fell to Ritchie at the Indigo Plateau after Charizard refused to battle. A league conference is not the same as becoming Champion. Alola is the first league Ash actually won.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`indigo`,`ash`]},{id:`ani-orange`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`The Orange Islands are an anime-only Kanto detour`,stat:`S2`,sub:`GS Ball errand. Not a main-series game region.`,why:`After Indigo, Ash sails the Orange Islands for Professor Oak and the GS Ball. That archipelago is not a core-game region. English called the block Adventures in the Orange Islands, while Japan still numbered it as the same Pocket Monsters show.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`indigo`,`ash`]},{id:`ani-johto`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Johto is seasons three through five in English`,stat:`GS`,sub:`Johto Journeys, League Champions, Master Quest.`,why:`Japan moved into Gold and Silver country in 1999. English split that into The Johto Journeys, Johto League Champions, and Master Quest. That is original series, still Satoshi, not a new lead.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`ash`,`indigo`]},{id:`ani-silver`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Ash reached the Silver Conference and lost to Harrison`,stat:`S5`,sub:`Johto League. Master Quest is the English label.`,why:`The Johto League is the Silver Conference. Ash makes a deep run and falls to Harrison from Hoenn, which tees up Advanced Generation. Japan aired that ending in 2002, and English Master Quest wrapped in 2003.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`ash`]},{id:`ani-charizard`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Ash's Charizard spent seasons refusing to listen`,stat:`OS`,sub:`Charmander to Charizard. Loyalty came later.`,why:`Ash's Charmander evolved into Charmeleon and then Charizard, then ignored him in gyms and at Indigo. Charicific Valley and later battles repaired that. It is an original-series character arc, not Roy's later Fire starter.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`ash`,`indigo`]},{id:`ani-squirtle`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Ash caught a Squirtle from the Squirtle Squad`,stat:`EP012`,sub:`A Kanto catch. Not the professor's starter.`,why:`Ash's Squirtle was the leader of a sunglasses gang in episode 12, not Oak's leftover starter. It later returns to squad duty. That catch is original-series Kanto, and Horizons does not reuse that Squirtle.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`ash`,`indigo`]},{id:`ani-porygon`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Electric Soldier Porygon aired once in Japan`,stat:`EP038`,sub:`December 16, 1997. Never dubbed. Never rerun.`,why:`EP038, Dennō Senshi Porygon, aired in Japan on December 16, 1997. Flashing red and blue frames sent hundreds of viewers to hospitals. The episode was pulled worldwide and was never dubbed in English.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`indigo`]},{id:`ani-hiatus`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`The Porygon incident put the Japan show on hiatus`,stat:`1998`,sub:`Four months off. Returned April 16, 1998.`,why:`After December 16, 1997, TV Tokyo halted the series for about four months. It returned on April 16, 1998 with new lighting rules and a viewer warning. The US premiere was still months away, in September 1998, so English viewers never lived that gap.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`indigo`]},{id:`ani-os-end`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`The original series ran Japan 1997 to 2002`,stat:`OS`,sub:`US 1998 to 2003. Then Advanced Generation.`,why:`Japan's original Pocket Monsters block runs April 1, 1997 to November 14, 2002. English seasons 1 through 5 cover that, ending Master Quest in 2003. Advanced Generation is the next Japanese series, not a fifth year of Indigo League.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`indigo`,`ash`]},{id:`ani-st-anne`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Some US markets saw Battle Aboard the St. Anne first`,stat:`EP015`,sub:`A 1998 sneak. The real start is still I Choose You.`,why:`The US sneak preview in 1998 was EP015, Battle Aboard the St. Anne, not episode one. Then syndication ran Pokémon - I Choose You! as the proper start. If someone says the first English episode was a cruise ship, they mean the sneak, not Japan's April 1, 1997 premiere.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`indigo`,`4kids`]},{id:`ani-kids-wb`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Kids' WB carried the 4Kids dub after syndication`,stat:`WB`,sub:`Saturday morning. The theater cards even say Kids' WB.`,why:`After the 1998 syndicated launch, Kids' WB became the US TV home for the 4Kids dub. The First Movie promo cards are stamped Kids' WB Presents Pokémon The First Movie. That stamp is 1999 US merch, not a Japan CoroCoro insert.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`4kids`,`indigo`]},{id:`ani-misty-togepi`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Misty's Togepi hatched from an egg Ash found`,stat:`EP050`,sub:`Original series. Togepi is hers, not Ash's mascot forever.`,why:`An egg Ash found hatches into Togepi, who imprints on Misty. That is original-series Kanto, around EP050. Togepi later evolves in her care after she leaves the party.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`ash`,`indigo`]},{id:`ani-gs-ball`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`The GS Ball was an original-series MacGuffin`,stat:`OS`,sub:`Kurt in Azalea. The plot quietly dropped it.`,why:`Ash carries the GS Ball through Orange Islands into Johto for Kurt. The weekly show then stops using it. It is not Liko's pendant and not Roy's ancient Poké Ball.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`ash`,`indigo`]},{id:`ani-ho-oh`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Ash sees Ho-Oh in episode one, before Johto exists on TV`,stat:`EP001`,sub:`A rainbow bird. Not a gym badge. Not a catch.`,why:`EP001 ends with a Ho-Oh in the sky, years before the Johto series. It is a promise shot, not a caught legendary. I Choose You! the 2017 movie uses Ho-Oh again in another timeline.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`ash`,`indigo`]},{id:`ani-who`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Who's that Pokémon was a 4Kids bumper, not a Japan quiz`,stat:`BUMPER`,sub:`Commercial break. Silhouette, then the reveal.`,why:`The English dub put a silhouette quiz before the second act. Japan did not run that exact bumper. Later English releases sometimes dropped it, because it is packaging, not an episode code.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`4kids`,`indigo`]},{id:`ani-oak-ratings`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Professor Oak's lecture is an English-broadcast extra`,stat:`OAK`,sub:`Who's that Pokémon. Japan used different bumpers.`,why:`English TV added Who's that Pokémon and Oak's lecture bumpers. Japan used its own eyecatches. A US kid who learned Porygon from a bumper still never saw EP038.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`indigo`,`4kids`]},{id:`ani-motto`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Team Rocket's English motto is a 4Kids rewrite`,stat:`MOTTO`,sub:`Prepare for trouble. Japan used different verse.`,why:`Jessie and James' English motto, Prepare for trouble, is the 4Kids version of a Japanese introduction. Horizons does not open weeks with that speech because Team Rocket is not the weekly villain. A motto you remember is an Ash-era dub memory.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`4kids`,`indigo`]},{id:`ani-blast-off`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Blasting off again is an Ash-era gag, not Horizons`,stat:`OS`,sub:`Team Rocket flies. Explorers do not say that line.`,why:`Jessie, James, and Meowth blasting off is the original series' end-of-act button. Horizons' Explorers lose battles without that catchphrase. If a still has a twinkle in the sky and a motto, you are in the Ash years.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`ash`,`indigo`,`horizons`]},{id:`ani-ag`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Advanced Generation moved Ash to Hoenn in 2002`,stat:`AG`,sub:`Japan November 21, 2002. English Advanced in 2003.`,why:`Pokémon the Series: Ruby and Sapphire, called Advanced Generation in Japan, premiered November 21, 2002. English branded it Advanced, then Advanced Challenge, Advanced Battle, and Battle Frontier. Hoenn is the region, not Johto season six.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`ash`]},{id:`ani-may-max`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`May and Max joined Ash for the Hoenn years`,stat:`AG`,sub:`Haruka and Masato. Contests, not gyms, for May.`,why:`May starts as a new Hoenn trainer, and her little brother Max tags along. Japan names them Haruka and Masato. May aims at Pokémon Contests while Ash aims at gyms, and Misty does not travel Hoenn with them.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`ash`]},{id:`ani-ag-frontier`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Battle Frontier is still Advanced Generation`,stat:`S9`,sub:`Kanto again, after the Hoenn League.`,why:`After the Hoenn League, Ash challenges the Battle Frontier back in Kanto. English called that stretch season 9, while Japan still labeled it Advanced Generation. It is not Diamond and Pearl yet.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`ash`]},{id:`ani-ag-league`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Ash lost the Hoenn League to Tyson`,stat:`AG`,sub:`Ever Grande Conference. Same pattern as Indigo.`,why:`Ash collects Hoenn badges, reaches the Ever Grande Conference, and loses to Tyson. English viewers saw it across Advanced Battle. A conference loss is not a Champion title.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`ash`]},{id:`ani-drew`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Drew is May's contest rival, not Ash's gym rival`,stat:`AG`,sub:`Shū in Japan. Ribbons, not badges.`,why:`Drew competes in Pokémon Contests with a Roselia. May's Hoenn story is coordinators and ribbons, while Ash's story is gyms. They share a traveling party, not a goal.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`ash`]},{id:`ani-ag-end`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Advanced Generation ended in Japan in 2006`,stat:`AG192`,sub:`Home Is Where the Start Is. Then Sinnoh.`,why:`The last Advanced Generation episode, AG192, aired in Japan on September 14, 2006. Diamond and Pearl starts the same week in Japan. English Battle Frontier lagged into 2007.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`ash`]},{id:`ani-taj`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`The dub left 4Kids during Battle Frontier`,stat:`2006`,sub:`Pokémon USA, TAJ Productions. New Ash, new Brock.`,why:`Around English season 9, Pokémon USA took dubbing in-house, so Sarah Natochenny became Ash and Bill Rogers became Brock. Japan did not recast Satoshi. A voice change in Battle Frontier is an English production change, not a new Japanese lead.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`ash`,`4kids`]},{id:`ani-dp`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Diamond and Pearl sent Ash to Sinnoh in 2006`,stat:`DP`,sub:`Japan September 28, 2006. Dawn replaces May.`,why:`Pokémon the Series: Diamond and Pearl premiered in Japan on September 28, 2006. English split it into Diamond and Pearl, Battle Dimension, Galactic Battles, and Sinnoh League Victors. Dawn is the new companion, and Brock stays.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`ash`]},{id:`ani-dawn`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Dawn is a Sinnoh coordinator with a Piplup`,stat:`DAWN`,sub:`Hikari in Japan. Contests, like May before her.`,why:`Dawn starts in Twinleaf Town and travels with Ash and Brock through Sinnoh. Japan calls her Hikari. Her first partner is Piplup, and she wants to be a top coordinator, not a Gym Leader.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`ash`]},{id:`ani-paul`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Paul is Ash's Sinnoh rival, colder than Gary`,stat:`PAUL`,sub:`Shinji in Japan. The League rematch is the point.`,why:`Paul releases Pokémon he calls weak and battles to win, not to befriend. Japan names him Shinji. Ash's full-team loss and later Sinnoh League rematch are the Diamond and Pearl spine.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`ash`]},{id:`ani-galactic`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Team Galactic is the Sinnoh villain plot`,stat:`DP`,sub:`Cyrus, Dialga, Palkia. Not Team Rocket's weekly gag.`,why:`Team Galactic's Cyrus hunts Dialga and Palkia across Diamond and Pearl. English titled a season Galactic Battles, while Team Rocket still appears weekly. Those are two different antagonist layers.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`ash`]},{id:`ani-sinnoh-league`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Ash lost the Sinnoh League to Tobias and Darkrai`,stat:`DP`,sub:`Lily of the Valley. English: Sinnoh League Victors.`,why:`Ash reaches the Sinnoh League, beats Paul in a six-on-six, then falls to Tobias, who leads with a Darkrai. Japan aired that in 2010. English called the last block Sinnoh League Victors.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`ash`]},{id:`ani-dp-end`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Diamond and Pearl wrapped in Japan in 2010`,stat:`DP191`,sub:`English lagged into September 2010.`,why:`The Sinnoh series ended in Japan on September 9, 2010, and Black and White began two weeks later. English Sinnoh League Victors closed the same year. Four English season names still equal one Japanese series: Diamond and Pearl.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`ash`]},{id:`ani-bw`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Black and White reset Ash in Unova in 2010`,stat:`BW`,sub:`Best Wishes in Japan. New traveling team.`,why:`Japan titled the Unova series Best Wishes and premiered it September 23, 2010. Ash leaves most of his old team at Oak's and starts over with a new Unova catch list. English called it Black and White, then Rival Destinies, then Adventures in Unova and Beyond.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`ash`]},{id:`ani-iris-cilan`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Iris and Cilan travel Unova with Ash`,stat:`BW`,sub:`Iris wants Dragon Master. Cilan is a Striaton gym lead.`,why:`Iris and Cilan replace Dawn and Brock as the weekly party. Cilan is one of Striaton's trio of gym leaders and a Pokémon Connoisseur. They are Unova companions, not Kalos, not Paldea.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`ash`]},{id:`ani-plasma`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Team Plasma is the Unova villain group`,stat:`BW`,sub:`N and Ghetsis. Rocket still does the comic relief.`,why:`Team Plasma's liberation plot, N, and Ghetsis arrive in Best Wishes, while Team Rocket remains the gag trio. Plasma is Unova's organization. The Explorers are Horizons.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`ash`]},{id:`ani-unova-league`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Ash lost the Unova League to Cameron`,stat:`BW`,sub:`Vertress Conference. Still no Champion title.`,why:`Ash's Unova League run ends against Cameron. That is Best Wishes, Japan 2012, English Rival Destinies into Adventures in Unova. Alola is still the first league trophy.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`ash`]},{id:`ani-bw-end`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Black and White ended in Japan in 2013`,stat:`BW`,sub:`Then XY. Kalos is the next series, not Unova 2.`,why:`Best Wishes closed in Japan on September 26, 2013. Pokémon the Series: XY started October 17, 2013. English Adventures in Unova and Beyond is the last Unova season name.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`ash`]},{id:`ani-xy`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`XY moved Ash to Kalos in 2013`,stat:`XY`,sub:`Japan October 17, 2013. Serena, Clemont, Bonnie.`,why:`Pokémon the Series: XY premiered in Japan on October 17, 2013. English used XY, then Kalos Quest, then XYZ. Mega Evolution is the Kalos gimmick on TV, years before Horizons brought it back.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`ash`]},{id:`ani-serena`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Serena is Ash's Kalos companion and a performer`,stat:`SERENA`,sub:`Same Japanese name. Showcases, not gyms.`,why:`Serena knew Ash from Pallet Town camp, then travels Kalos aiming at Pokémon Showcases. Japan kept the name Serena. She is XY, not Dawn, not Liko, and Horizons does not continue that party.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`ash`]},{id:`ani-clemont-bonnie`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Clemont is Lumiose Gym. Bonnie is his little sister`,stat:`XY`,sub:`Citron and Eureka in Japan. Kalos only.`,why:`Clemont runs the Lumiose City Gym and invents gadgets, while Bonnie tries to find his Dedenne a trainer. Japan names them Citron and Eureka. They belong to XY and XYZ, not the Rising Volt Tacklers.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`ash`]},{id:`ani-mega-xy`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Mega Evolution first hit the weekly show in XY`,stat:`MEGA`,sub:`Kalos, 2013. Horizons brought it back in 2025.`,why:`XY is where the TV series first treated Mega Evolution as a regular tool, matching X and Y. That is a 2013 Kalos beat. Roy's Mega Lucario in 2025 Horizons is a later revival, not Ash's Kalos party continuing.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`ash`,`lucario`]},{id:`ani-greninja`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Ash-Greninja is a Kalos anime form`,stat:`XY`,sub:`Bond Phenomenon. Not every Greninja. Not a TCG pull.`,why:`Ash's Froakie becomes Greninja and, in battle with Ash, takes on the Ash-Greninja look. The games added a related form around X and Y. A chase Greninja card is a printing, not that TV form's drop rate.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`ash`]},{id:`ani-xyz`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`XYZ is the last Kalos season in English`,stat:`XYZ`,sub:`Team Flare. Alain. Serena's goodbye.`,why:`English XYZ covers the Team Flare climax, Alain, and the Lumiose Conference. Japan still filed it under XY. The series ended in Japan on October 27, 2016, and Sun and Moon began three weeks later.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`ash`]},{id:`ani-serena-kiss`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Serena kissed Ash at the end of XYZ`,stat:`XY140`,sub:`Japan 2016. English XYZ lagged into 2017.`,why:`The Kalos send-off has Serena kiss Ash before she leaves for Hoenn. Japan aired that in 2016. English XYZ reached it in 2017.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`ash`]},{id:`ani-alain`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Alain beat Ash in the Lumiose Conference final`,stat:`XY`,sub:`Mega Charizard X versus Ash-Greninja.`,why:`Ash reaches the Kalos League final and loses to Alain, Mega Charizard X versus Ash-Greninja. Japan aired that in 2016, English XYZ in 2017. Still not a Champion title; Alola is next for that.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`ash`]},{id:`ani-flare`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Team Flare is the Kalos organization in XYZ`,stat:`XYZ`,sub:`Lysandre. Not Team Rocket. Not the Explorers.`,why:`Team Flare and Lysandre drive the XYZ climax around Zygarde and Kalos. Team Rocket still pops in, and those are different groups. Horizons' Explorers are a later show's villains.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`ash`]},{id:`ani-sm`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Sun and Moon sent Ash to school in Alola`,stat:`SM`,sub:`Japan November 17, 2016. A classroom, not a gym road.`,why:`Pokémon the Series: Sun and Moon premiered in Japan on November 17, 2016. Ash enrolls at the Pokémon School on Melemele Island with Kiawe, Lana, Mallow, Sophocles, and Lillie. English split it into Sun and Moon, Ultra Adventures, and Ultra Legends.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`ash`]},{id:`ani-alola-win`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Alola is the first league Ash actually won`,stat:`SM`,sub:`Manalo Conference. Champion of Alola, 2019 Japan.`,why:`After Kanto, Johto, Hoenn, Sinnoh, Unova, and Kalos losses, Ash wins the first Alola League and becomes Alola Champion. Japan aired that climax in 2019, late Ultra Legends, and English lagged months. A Champion title here is Alola only, not the later World Championship.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`ash`]},{id:`ani-kukui`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Professor Kukui is Ash's Alola teacher and housemate`,stat:`SM`,sub:`The Masked Royal is the same man. Alola only.`,why:`Ash lives at Professor Kukui's during Sun and Moon. Kukui also battles as the Masked Royal. He is the Alola professor on TV, not Oak, not Cerise, not Friede.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`ash`]},{id:`ani-zmove`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Z-Moves are the Alola gimmick on the weekly show`,stat:`SM`,sub:`Z-Rings and crystals. Not Mega. Not Tera.`,why:`Sun and Moon uses Z-Moves the way the Alola games do: a Z-Ring, a crystal, one big attack. That is 2016–2019 Alola TV. Mega Evolution was Kalos, and Terastal is Paldea and Horizons.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`ash`]},{id:`ani-sm-end`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Sun and Moon ended in Japan in November 2019`,stat:`SM146`,sub:`Then Journeys. Same Ash, new lab in Kanto.`,why:`The Alola series closed in Japan on November 3, 2019, and Journeys began two weeks later. English Ultra Legends lagged into 2020. The school cast does not become the Journeys party.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`ash`]},{id:`ani-lillie`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Lillie could not touch Pokémon at the start of Sun and Moon`,stat:`SM`,sub:`Alola school. Snowy the Vulpix. Not Liko.`,why:`Lillie is an Alola classmate who at first cannot touch Pokémon, then bonds with a Vulpix named Snowy. Similar name to Liko, different show, different decade. Sun and Moon is 2016 Japan; Horizons is 2023.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`ash`,`liko`]},{id:`ani-kiawe`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Kiawe, Lana, Mallow, and Sophocles are Alola classmates`,stat:`SM`,sub:`Pokémon School. Not the Rising Volt Tacklers.`,why:`Sun and Moon's weekly kids are Kiawe, Lana, Mallow, Sophocles, Lillie, and Ash. Horizons' kids are Liko, Roy, Dot, later Ult. A group shot of seven on a beach is probably Alola.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`ash`]},{id:`ani-jn`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Journeys started in Japan on November 17, 2019`,stat:`JN`,sub:`Pocket Monsters again. English: Journeys in 2020.`,why:`The seventh Japanese series, titled Pocket Monsters again, premiered November 17, 2019. English called it Pokémon Journeys, then Master Journeys, then Ultimate Journeys. The US debut was June 12, 2020.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`ash`]},{id:`ani-goh`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Goh is a catcher based at Cerise Laboratory`,stat:`GOH`,sub:`Go in Japan. He wants Mew. Ash wants Leon.`,why:`Goh travels with Ash in Journeys but his goal is to catch Pokémon, especially Mew, not to collect gym badges. Professor Cerise's lab in Vermilion is home base. Goh is Journeys, not a Horizons Rising Volt Tackler.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`ash`]},{id:`ani-wcs`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`The World Coronation Series is Ash's Journeys ladder`,stat:`JN018`,sub:`Ranks toward Leon. Not a regional gym circuit.`,why:`Ash enters the World Coronation Series in JN018 to climb toward Leon, Galar's Champion. Classes run from Normal up to Master, then the Masters Eight. Japan aired that in 2020, and English Journeys later in 2020.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`ash`]},{id:`ani-leon`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Leon is the undefeated Monarch Ash has to beat`,stat:`LEON`,sub:`Dande in Japan. Galar Champion on the TV show.`,why:`Leon opens Journeys by crushing Ash, then sits at the top of the World Coronation Series. Japan names him Dande. The Masters Eight final is the rematch, and he is a Journeys rival, not a Horizons Explorer.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`ash`]},{id:`ani-champion`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Ash became World Champion in Partners in Time`,stat:`JN131`,sub:`Japan November 11, 2022. US June 23, 2023.`,why:`JN131 is the Masters Eight final against Leon, where Ash wins and becomes World Champion. Japan aired it November 11, 2022, and the US got that block on June 23, 2023. Alola Champion and World Champion are two different trophies, years apart.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`ash`]},{id:`ani-masters-eight`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`The Masters Eight mixed Champions from older regions`,stat:`JN`,sub:`Iris, Cynthia, Leon, Lance, Steven, Diantha, Alain, Ash.`,why:`The Master-class eight included Unova's Iris, Sinnoh's Cynthia, Galar's Leon, Kanto-Johto's Lance, Hoenn's Steven, Kalos's Diantha, Alain, and Ash. Japan aired those matches in 2022. English Ultimate Journeys put them in 2023.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`ash`]},{id:`ani-jn-end`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Ash's last episode aired in Japan on March 24, 2023`,stat:`JN147`,sub:`The Rainbow and the Pokémon Master. US September 8.`,why:`JN147 closed Ash's run in Japan on March 24, 2023. The English finale reached the US on September 8, 2023. Horizons began in Japan three weeks after that Japan finale, on April 14, while the US still had months of Ash left.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`ash`]},{id:`ani-jn-lag`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`English Journeys lagged Japan by months, sometimes a year`,stat:`JN`,sub:`Japan 2019–2023. US 2020–2023. Not simulcast.`,why:`Journeys premiered in Japan in November 2019 and in the US in June 2020. The championship and finale blocks landed in the US in 2023, after Japan had finished. Netflix and TV windows are catch-up, not the Japan Friday night slot.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`ash`]},{id:`ani-project-mew`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Project Mew is Goh's side plot, not Ash's league`,stat:`JN`,sub:`A catcher trial. Mew is the target.`,why:`Goh joins Project Mew, a research trial aimed at finding Mew. That runs beside Ash's World Coronation climb. Two goals, one traveling pair.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`ash`]},{id:`ani-goh-cinderace`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Goh's first partner is Scorbunny, then Cinderace`,stat:`JN`,sub:`Journeys, not Horizons. Roy's Fire starter is Fuecoco.`,why:`Goh partners with Scorbunny, then Raboot, then Cinderace while trying to catch everything else. That is Journeys. Roy's Fuecoco is Horizons, a different Fire starter on a different show.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`ash`,`fuecoco`]},{id:`ani-chloe`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Chloe is Professor Cerise's daughter in Journeys`,stat:`CHLOE`,sub:`Eevee. Lab kid. Not a Horizons lead.`,why:`Chloe studies at her father's Vermilion lab and partners with an Eevee. Journeys only. Lab kids from 2019–2023 do not move onto the Brave Olivine.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`ash`]},{id:`ani-hz-start`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Pokémon Horizons premiered in Japan on April 14, 2023`,stat:`HZ001`,sub:`New leads. Ash's run had ended three weeks earlier.`,why:`Horizons opened in Japan on April 14, 2023 with a one-hour special, HZ001 and HZ002. Ash's last episode had aired March 24. This is a new Japanese series with new leads, not season 26 of Satoshi.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`horizons`,`liko`]},{id:`ani-hz-us`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`US Netflix got Horizons on March 7, 2024`,stat:`2024`,sub:`328 days after Japan. The UK had iPlayer in December.`,why:`Japan started Horizons April 14, 2023. US Netflix dropped the first batch on March 7, 2024, after a delay from a February plan. BBC iPlayer in the UK had episodes in December 2023.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`horizons`,`liko`]},{id:`ani-liko`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Liko is the new lead, not a guest in Ash's show`,stat:`LIKO`,sub:`Indoors kid, pendant, Sprigatito. Horizons from HZ001.`,why:`Liko starts Horizons as a student from the Paldea region with a mysterious pendant. She is a protagonist, not Ash's traveling kid. Japan and English both keep the name Liko.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`horizons`,`liko`,`sprigatito`]},{id:`ani-roy`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Roy is Liko's fellow lead with an ancient Poké Ball`,stat:`ROY`,sub:`Fuecoco first. Later a Lucario that Mega Evolves.`,why:`Roy meets Liko in Paldea, partners with Fuecoco, and carries an ancient Poké Ball tied to a black Rayquaza. He is a Horizons lead, not Ash. The 2025 Mega Voltage arc adds his Shiny Lucario.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`horizons`,`fuecoco`,`lucario`]},{id:`ani-sprigatito`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Liko started with Sprigatito, the Paldea Grass starter`,stat:`HZ003`,sub:`Her partner. Not a game save. Not Ash's.`,why:`Liko's first partner is Sprigatito, which later becomes Floragato and Meowscarada. That is the Horizons team, chosen by the writers. Scarlet and Violet let you pick that starter; the show already picked it for her.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`horizons`,`liko`,`sprigatito`]},{id:`ani-fuecoco`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Roy started with Fuecoco, the Paldea Fire starter`,stat:`HZ005`,sub:`Then Crocalor. The show chose. Your Scarlet save did not.`,why:`Roy's first partner is Fuecoco, found as a stowaway with the Rising Volt Tacklers, then Crocalor. HZ005 is the Japan meeting. English Netflix put that in the first 2024 batch.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`horizons`,`fuecoco`]},{id:`ani-captain`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Captain Pikachu is Friede's, not Ash's`,stat:`CAP`,sub:`The ship's partner. Different Pikachu. Different show.`,why:`Captain Pikachu, Cap, belongs to Professor Friede on the Brave Olivine, and Ikue Ōtani voices him too. Ash's Pikachu is from the 1997–2023 show and does not transfer into Horizons as the lead's partner. Two Pikachu, two stories, same species.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`captain`,`horizons`]},{id:`ani-friede`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Friede is the Rising Volt Tacklers' professor`,stat:`FRIEDE`,sub:`Charizard, Cap, the Brave Olivine. Not Oak.`,why:`Friede captains the airship, battles with Charizard and Captain Pikachu, and mentors Liko and Roy. He is a Horizons professor, not Pallet Town's Oak and not Cerise. Mega Voltage later hides him and brings him back.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`captain`,`horizons`]},{id:`ani-rvt`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`The Rising Volt Tacklers are Horizons' crew, not Team Rocket`,stat:`RVT`,sub:`Airship family. Explorers are the villains.`,why:`The Rising Volt Tacklers sail the Brave Olivine: Friede, Orla, Murdock, Mollie, Ludlow, then Liko, Roy, and Dot. They are the good crew. The Explorers chase Liko's pendant.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`horizons`,`captain`]},{id:`ani-olivine`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`The Brave Olivine is the Horizons airship`,stat:`SHIP`,sub:`Home base. Not Pallet Town. Not Cerise Lab.`,why:`Horizons lives on an airship, the Brave Olivine, instead of a walking gym road. Orla keeps it flying. That vehicle is the series' geography, and Ash's Pallet Town is a different home.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`horizons`,`captain`]},{id:`ani-explorers`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`The Explorers are Horizons' villains`,stat:`HZ`,sub:`Amethio, Spinel, Gibeon. Not Jessie and James.`,why:`The Explorers want Liko's pendant and later Laquium. Amethio is the early face, with Spinel and Gibeon deeper in. Team Rocket does not fill that slot.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`horizons`,`liko`]},{id:`ani-dot`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Dot streams as Nidothing and partners with Quaxly`,stat:`DOT`,sub:`Third Paldea starter on the ship. Not a lead at first.`,why:`Dot hides on the Brave Olivine, streams as Nidothing, then catches Quaxly in HZ016. She completes the Paldea starter trio with Liko's Sprigatito and Roy's Fuecoco. Japan aired that August 4, 2023, and English Netflix in 2024.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`horizons`]},{id:`ani-terapagos`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Liko's pendant was Terapagos`,stat:`HZ`,sub:`Pagogo. Paldea's turtle, not a Kanto fossil.`,why:`The mystery in Liko's pendant is Terapagos, nicknamed Pagogo on the show. That ties Horizons to Scarlet and Violet lore and to the ancient adventurer Lucius. US Netflix reached that reveal months after Japan.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`horizons`,`liko`]},{id:`ani-lucius`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Lucius is the ancient adventurer behind the Six Heroes`,stat:`HZ`,sub:`Black Rayquaza, Arboliva, and more. Old Paldea myth.`,why:`Lucius, the Ancient Adventurer, left Pokémon in ancient Poké Balls that Liko and Roy collect as the Six Heroes. Roy's black Rayquaza is one. This is Horizons' long plot, not a Kanto legendary cameo.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`horizons`]},{id:`ani-hz-series`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`The West treats Horizons as a new show, not season 26`,stat:`HZ`,sub:`Ash's 25 English seasons closed. Then Liko and Roy.`,why:`Pokémon the Series in English is the 25-season Ash run. Pokémon Horizons: The Series is filed as a separate animated series. Season 26 on a US list is Horizons season 1, not another Ash year.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`horizons`,`ash`]},{id:`ani-netflix-lag`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`US Netflix Horizons runs months behind Japan`,stat:`LAG`,sub:`Batches, not Friday simulcast. UK iPlayer is another clock.`,why:`Japan airs Horizons weekly on TV Tokyo. US Netflix drops dubbed batches: first season March 2024, later arcs in parts. If Japan is on Wonder Voyage, US Netflix may still be finishing Rising Hope.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`horizons`]},{id:`ani-mega-voltage`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Mega Voltage is a 2025 Horizons arc, one year later`,stat:`HZ090`,sub:`Japan April 11 to September 19, 2025. Not a TCG set.`,why:`Mega Voltage is Horizons episodes HZ090–HZ111, Japan April 11 to September 19, 2025, set a year after Laqua. Liko, Roy, and Dot are older, and Ult joins. English filed a lot of it under Rising Hope, and it is not a Mega Evolution card set.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`horizons`,`lucario`]},{id:`ani-roy-lucario`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Roy's Shiny Lucario is the Mega Evolution that returned`,stat:`MEGA`,sub:`Horizons 2025. Not Ash's Kalos Mega season.`,why:`Mega Evolution returned to the weekly show in 2025 during Horizons, and Roy's Shiny Lucario Mega Evolves. That is a new character's partner, not Ash-Greninja and not the 2005 Lucario movie. Kalos Mega TV and Horizons Mega TV are a decade apart.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`lucario`,`horizons`]},{id:`ani-ult`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Ult joins in Mega Voltage with a Sableye`,stat:`ULT`,sub:`New kid, 2025 Japan. English Rising Hope batches.`,why:`Ult is a new companion from the Mega Voltage arc, chasing strong Pokémon with his Sableye, which can Mega Evolve. Japan met him in April 2025. US Netflix put him in Rising Hope parts in 2026.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`horizons`]},{id:`ani-indigo-academy`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Liko attends Indigo Academy during Mega Voltage`,stat:`HZ090`,sub:`A school year after Laqua. Not Indigo League 1997.`,why:`Mega Voltage opens with Liko at Indigo Academy while Roy travels with Captain Pikachu. The name echoes Kanto's Indigo Plateau, but this is a 2025 Horizons school, not Ash's 1997 league. Japan, April 11, 2025; English Rising Hope, January 6, 2026.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`horizons`,`liko`,`captain`]},{id:`ani-rising-hope`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Rising Hope is the English name for Horizons season 3`,stat:`S3`,sub:`Japan had Mega Voltage underway in 2025.`,why:`English Netflix labels this chunk Pokémon Horizons: Season 3—Rising Hope. Japan was already airing Mega Voltage in 2025 and kept going into 2026. Season 3 in English is a packaging name, not a third Japanese series after Ash.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`horizons`]},{id:`ani-rising-hope-p1`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Rising Hope Part 1 hit US Netflix on January 6, 2026`,stat:`JAN 6`,sub:`Catch-up. Japan had been in that story since April 2025.`,why:`Netflix US dropped Rising Hope Part 1 on January 6, 2026, including the Mega Voltage open with older Liko, Roy, Dot, Ult, and Roy's Lucario. Japan had started that arc April 11, 2025. Nine months is lag, not a new episode that night in Tokyo.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`horizons`,`lucario`]},{id:`ani-rising-hope-p2`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Rising Hope Part 2 landed on Netflix on March 20, 2026`,stat:`MAR 20`,sub:`Ten more English episodes. Japan was still ahead.`,why:`Part 2 of Rising Hope went to US Netflix on March 20, 2026, about ten episodes, still the Mega Voltage story. BBC iPlayer in the UK had a batch in late February. Three clocks: Tokyo, London, Los Angeles.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`horizons`]},{id:`ani-rising-hope-p3`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Rising Hope Part 3 arrived on Netflix on June 26, 2026`,stat:`JUN 26`,sub:`Blueberry Academy. Japan had already finished season 3.`,why:`US Netflix added Rising Hope Part 3 on June 26, 2026, including Blueberry Academy and BB League Elite Four training. Japan had already closed the season 3 broadcast on May 1, 2026. You were catching up in June, not watching the Japan finale live.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`horizons`,`liko`]},{id:`ani-rising-hope-p4`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Rising Hope Part 4 hits Netflix on September 18, 2026`,stat:`SEP 18`,sub:`English catch-up. Japan is already on Wonder Voyage.`,why:`Netflix US scheduled Rising Hope Part 4 for September 18, 2026, the Laqua showdown dubbed. Japan finished that English season on May 1, 2026 and started Wonder Voyage on May 22. Same calendar day as Japan's HZ150, two different episodes in two regions.`,asOf:`2026-09-18`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`horizons`,`sprigatito`]},{id:`ani-wonder-voyage`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Wonder Voyage started in Japan on May 22, 2026`,stat:`HZ137`,sub:`Kalos and Paldea. Navi and a Ditto. Horizons did not end.`,why:`Wonder Voyage is the next Horizons arc, Japan May 22, 2026, after the Laquium crisis. Liko, Roy, Dot, Ult, and new companion Navi with a Ditto keep going. US Netflix was still rolling Rising Hope parts.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`horizons`,`liko`]},{id:`ani-navi`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Navi is the Wonder Voyage companion with a Ditto`,stat:`HZ137`,sub:`From Lumiose. Japan May 2026. English later.`,why:`Wonder Voyage's key visual adds Navi, a girl from Lumiose City, and a Ditto. That arc started May 22, 2026 in Japan. English naming and Netflix dating lag, as they did for Rising Hope.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`horizons`]},{id:`ani-hz150`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`HZ150 is a Pokémon GO crossover in Japan on September 18`,stat:`HZ150`,sub:`Professor Willow on TV. Not your GO map event.`,why:`Japan's HZ150, September 18, 2026, puts Liko, Roy, Friede, and Captain Pikachu with Professor Willow, PokéStops, Meltan, and a Kecleon gag. Pokémon GO ran a matching Horizons event September 16–22. One is a cartoon episode, and one is the phone game.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`horizons`,`captain`]},{id:`ani-willow`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Professor Willow's anime debut is that Horizons crossover`,stat:`HZ150`,sub:`GO's professor, 2026 TV. Oak still belongs to Kanto.`,why:`Willow has been the Pokémon GO professor on phones since 2016. HZ150 is his weekly-series debut, Japan first on September 18, 2026. He is not Professor Oak and not Friede.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`horizons`,`captain`]},{id:`ani-hz-lag-teach`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`A Horizons episode code is the Japan number, not Netflix part`,stat:`HZ`,sub:`HZ150 is Japan. Rising Hope Part 4 is a US pack.`,why:`HZ numbers count from the April 14, 2023 Japan start, while Netflix parts are English batch names. Rising Hope Part 4 on September 18, 2026 is not HZ150. HZ150 that same day in Japan is the Willow crossover.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`horizons`]},{id:`ani-liko-evo`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Liko's Sprigatito did not stay a starter`,stat:`HZ`,sub:`Floragato, then Meowscarada. Still her partner.`,why:`Horizons lets Liko's partner evolve on a character schedule, not on your friendship stat. Floragato and later Meowscarada are still Liko's. A Paldea line on TV is not proof your Sprigatito will mega or tera the same way.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`horizons`,`liko`,`sprigatito`]},{id:`ani-dot-quax`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Dot's Quaxly evolved all the way to Quaquaval`,stat:`HZ128`,sub:`Japan 2026. The Paldea trio fully evolved on the show.`,why:`HZ128 has Dot's Quaxwell become Quaquaval. By then the younger Rising Volt Tacklers' Paldea partners were fully evolved. Japan aired that during the later Rising Again stretch, and English Netflix batches trailed.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`horizons`]},{id:`ani-black-rayquaza`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Roy's ancient ball holds a black Rayquaza`,stat:`HZ`,sub:`One of Lucius' Six Heroes. Not Ash's Rayquaza cameo.`,why:`The black Rayquaza is Lucius' Hero, tied to Roy's ancient Poké Ball. It is a Horizons legendary plot, not a Journeys guest battle. TV legendaries are story, not pull rates.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`horizons`,`fuecoco`]},{id:`ani-amethio`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Amethio is the first Explorer who hunts Liko's pendant`,stat:`HZ001`,sub:`Japan April 14, 2023. English Netflix March 2024.`,why:`HZ001 and HZ002 introduce Amethio attacking Liko at school for the pendant. Japan aired that April 14, 2023, and US Netflix on March 7, 2024. He is an Explorer, not James and not Gary.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`horizons`,`liko`]},{id:`ani-spinel`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Spinel is the Explorer who keeps coming back for Laquium`,stat:`HZ`,sub:`Beheeyem and Umbreon. A long-game villain.`,why:`Spinel steals Liko's pendant early, then remains a Horizons antagonist through Laqua and Mega Voltage. He is not Giovanni and not a Team Rocket grunt. English Rising Hope still has him because Japan built that rivalry across 2023–2026.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`horizons`]},{id:`ani-murdock`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Murdock cooks on the Brave Olivine. Dot is his niece`,stat:`HZ`,sub:`Ship family. Not Brock. Not Team Rocket's chef gag.`,why:`Murdock is the Rising Volt Tacklers' cook and Dot's uncle. Brock cooked in the Ash years: same job, different show. Do not caption him as Brock because someone is holding a pan.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`horizons`]},{id:`ani-orla`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Orla is the engineer who keeps the Brave Olivine in the air`,stat:`HZ`,sub:`Metagross. Machinery. Horizons crew, not a gym leader.`,why:`Orla maintains the airship with a Metagross. She is Rising Volt Tacklers staff. Clemont invented gadgets in Kalos, a similar science vibe on a different series.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`horizons`,`captain`]},{id:`ani-mollie`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Mollie is the ship's doctor on the Brave Olivine`,stat:`HZ`,sub:`Nurse job. Brock later studied this. Different person.`,why:`Mollie treats Pokémon on the Rising Volt Tacklers' ship. Brock left the Ash show to become a Pokémon doctor. Horizons did not recast Brock as Mollie.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`horizons`]},{id:`ani-ludlow`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Ludlow fishes off the Brave Olivine with a Quagsire`,stat:`HZ`,sub:`Crew elder. Not Professor Oak in a hat.`,why:`Ludlow is the older Rising Volt Tackler who fishes and stays calm when the ship is chaos. He is not Oak and not a Kanto extra. Horizons built a whole adult crew so Liko and Roy are not walking a gym road alone.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`horizons`]},{id:`ani-pagogo`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Pagogo is the show's nickname for Liko's Terapagos`,stat:`HZ`,sub:`A nickname on TV. The species is still Terapagos.`,why:`Horizons calls Liko's Terapagos Pagogo the way a character gets a name on a species. It is not a regional form name in the games' Dex. TV nicknames are not TCG card titles.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`horizons`,`liko`]},{id:`ani-hz100`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`HZ100 rebuilt the Rising Volt Tacklers after Laqua`,stat:`HZ100`,sub:`Japan June 20, 2025. English in a later Rising Hope part.`,why:`HZ100, We Are the Rising Volt Tacklers, Japan June 20, 2025, puts Ult on the crew and hints Friede is alive. US Netflix placed that in a Rising Hope batch, not on June 20. Episode 100 of Horizons is not episode 100 of Ash.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`horizons`,`captain`]},{id:`ani-jp-s3-end`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Japan ended Horizons season 3 on May 1, 2026`,stat:`MAY 1`,sub:`47 English-counted episodes. Wonder Voyage started May 22.`,why:`The Rising Hope season 28 block ran in Japan from April 11, 2025 to May 1, 2026, 47 episodes. Wonder Voyage began three weeks later. US Netflix Part 4 in September 2026 is still finishing that closed Japan season.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`horizons`]},{id:`ani-search-laqua`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`The Search for Laqua is English Horizons season 2`,stat:`S2`,sub:`Japan 2024–2025. US Netflix lagged in parts.`,why:`English season 2, The Search for Laqua, covers Japan's 2024–2025 Friday episodes toward the Laqua climax. US Netflix released it in batches after Japan had moved into Mega Voltage. Japan's umbrella title stayed Pocket Monsters.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`horizons`]},{id:`ani-hz-s1-eps`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`English Horizons season 1 is 45 episodes, Japan 2023–2024`,stat:`S1`,sub:`April 14, 2023 to March 29, 2024 in Japan.`,why:`The first English Horizons season matches Japan's first year, 45 episodes, April 14, 2023 through March 29, 2024. US Netflix began March 7, 2024, almost at the Japan season's end. You can finish season 1 on Netflix and still be a year behind Tokyo.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`horizons`,`liko`]},{id:`ani-laqua`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Laqua is the Horizons place the Explorers and heroes fight over`,stat:`HZ`,sub:`Laquium is the threat. English season 2 hunted it.`,why:`The Search for Laqua is the English season 2 name. Laquium is the substance that makes Pokémon berserk in Mega Voltage. Japan told the Laqua battle in 2025, and English Rising Hope is the year-later fallout.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`horizons`]},{id:`ani-minori`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Minori Suzuki voices Liko. Yuka Terasaki voices Roy`,stat:`JP`,sub:`New leads, new Japanese cast. Not Rica Matsumoto.`,why:`Liko is Minori Suzuki and Roy is Yuka Terasaki. Rica Matsumoto's Satoshi ended in 2023. English Horizons also recast the leads rather than reuse Ash's voice.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`horizons`,`liko`]},{id:`ani-hz-uk`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`The UK often got Horizons on BBC iPlayer before US Netflix`,stat:`UK`,sub:`December 2023 UK. March 7, 2024 US Netflix.`,why:`BBC iPlayer and CBBC ran Horizons in the UK in December 2023. US Netflix waited until March 7, 2024 for season 1, and later parts kept trailing iPlayer on season 3. English is not one window.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`horizons`]},{id:`ani-cap-hat`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Captain Pikachu wears a cap. Ash's Pikachu does not`,stat:`CAP`,sub:`The hat is the tell. Two partners, one species.`,why:`Cap's hat is how stills separate Friede's partner from Ash's. GO even listed Pikachu wearing Cap's hat as a raid target in September 2026. If the Pikachu has a cap on an airship, it is Horizons.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`captain`,`ash`]},{id:`ani-ash-age`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Ash stays ten. Horizons lets Liko and Roy age a year`,stat:`AGE`,sub:`Mega Voltage is one year later. Satoshi never had that time skip.`,why:`Ash is ten from 1997 to 2023. Horizons explicitly skips a year after Laqua so Liko, Roy, and Dot are older in Mega Voltage. If the kids look taller in 2025 key art, you are in Horizons.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`ash`,`horizons`,`liko`]},{id:`ani-horizons-not-ash`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Ash does not come back as the Horizons lead`,stat:`2023`,sub:`Liko and Roy. Cameos are not a reboot of his run.`,why:`Horizons was billed as a new cast with no story obligation to Ash's 25-year road. Captain Pikachu is not a loophole that makes it Ash's show. The new count is HZ.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`horizons`,`ash`,`liko`]},{id:`ani-m1-jp`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`The first movie opened in Japan on July 18, 1998`,stat:`1998`,sub:`Mewtwo Strikes Back. Toho. Ash was still in Indigo.`,why:`Pocket Monsters the Movie: Mewtwo Strikes Back hit Japanese theaters July 18, 1998, while the TV show was still in its first series. US theaters opened more than a year later. A 1998 Japan movie date is not a 1998 English theatrical date.`,image:`/games/first-movie-poster.jpg`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`mewtwo`]},{id:`ani-m1-us`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`US theaters got Mewtwo Strikes Back in November 1999`,stat:`1999`,sub:`November 10 wide. Warner Bros. Japan had it in 1998.`,why:`Pokémon: The First Movie opened in the US on November 10, 1999, distributed by Warner Bros. Japan had already run it in July 1998. Only the first few films got a real US wide theatrical.`,image:`/games/first-movie-poster.jpg`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`mewtwo`]},{id:`ani-m1-promos`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`US first-movie tickets came with one of four promo cards`,stat:`1999`,sub:`Pikachu, Mewtwo, Electabuzz, or Dragonite. Not Ancient Mew.`,why:`Participating US theaters handed out a Wizards Black Star promo with a First Movie ticket from November 1999: Pikachu, Mewtwo, Electabuzz, or Dragonite, Kids' WB stamp. Ancient Mew is the next movie, The Power of One, in 2000.`,image:`/games/first-movie-mewtwo-promo.png`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`mewtwo`]},{id:`ani-m1-pikachu`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`The First Movie Pikachu promo is Wizards Black Star 4`,stat:`WIZ 4`,sub:`Theater 1999. One of four. Not a pack pull.`,why:`Pikachu LV.13, Wizards Promo 4, was in the 1999 US theater pool with Mewtwo, Electabuzz, and Dragonite. You did not choose which of the four you got. Japan did not hand out that Kids' WB stamped sheet.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`mewtwo`,`indigo`]},{id:`ani-m1-mewtwo-card`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`The First Movie Mewtwo promo is Wizards Black Star 3`,stat:`WIZ 3`,sub:`Sugimori art, Kids' WB stamp, November 1999 theaters.`,why:`Mewtwo LV.60, Wizards Promo 3, was one of the four US theater cards for Mewtwo Strikes Back, Ken Sugimori art with a gold Kids' WB stamp. A later Mewtwo promo came with the VHS. The movie is 1998 Japan / 1999 US.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`mewtwo`]},{id:`ani-m1-electabuzz`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Electabuzz was in the First Movie theater pack, not Ancient Mew week`,stat:`WIZ 2`,sub:`Wizards Promo 2. November 1999. Movie one.`,why:`Electabuzz LV.20, Wizards Promo 2, is a First Movie theater card from November 1999. People mix it with the 2000 Power of One bird cards. Electabuzz is movie one; Articuno, Zapdos, and Moltres are movie two after week one.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`mewtwo`]},{id:`ani-m1-dragonite`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Dragonite completed the four First Movie theater promos`,stat:`WIZ 5`,sub:`Wizards Promo 5. Same 1999 pool as Pikachu and Mewtwo.`,why:`Dragonite LV.43, Wizards Promo 5, is the fourth US theater promo for Mewtwo Strikes Back. All four share the November 1999 window and the Kids' WB stamp. None of them is Ancient Mew.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`mewtwo`]},{id:`ani-m1-short`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Pikachu's Vacation played before Mewtwo Strikes Back`,stat:`1998`,sub:`A Pikachu short. Most early movies had one.`,why:`Japanese and US prints of the first movie included the short Pikachu's Vacation. Later films kept that pattern for years. The short is not a TV episode number and not a TCG set.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`mewtwo`,`indigo`]},{id:`ani-ancient-mew`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Ancient Mew is a Power of One giveaway, not movie one`,stat:`2000`,sub:`US week one, July 21–27. Then the legendary birds.`,why:`US theaters gave Ancient Mew with The Power of One tickets in the first week, July 21–27, 2000. After that week you got Articuno, Zapdos, or Moltres. Japan put Ancient Mew in movie pamphlets from July 17, 1999. Movie one handed out Pikachu, Mewtwo, Electabuzz, or Dragonite instead.`,image:`/games/ancient-mew-promo.jpg`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`ancient-mew`]},{id:`ani-m2`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`The Power of One is movie two, Lugia, 1999 Japan`,stat:`1999`,sub:`Japan July 17, 1999. US July 21, 2000.`,why:`Pokémon the Movie 2000: The Power of One opened in Japan on July 17, 1999 and in US theaters on July 21, 2000. Lugia, Shamouti, and the three birds are that plot. Ancient Mew is this film's promo, not Mewtwo Strikes Back.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`mewtwo`]},{id:`ani-m3`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Movie three is Spell of the Unown, Entei, 2000 Japan`,stat:`2000`,sub:`Japan July 8, 2000. US April 6, 2001.`,why:`Pokémon 3: The Movie — Spell of the Unown opened in Japan on July 8, 2000 and in US theaters on April 6, 2001. Molly, the Unown, and an illusion Entei are the plot. It is the third wide US theatrical.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`]},{id:`ani-m4`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Pokémon 4Ever is Celebi, 2001 Japan, 2002 US`,stat:`2001`,sub:`Voice of the Forest. Miramax in the US.`,why:`Celebi: Voice of the Forest opened in Japan on July 7, 2001. The US theatrical, titled Pokémon 4Ever, came October 11, 2002 through Miramax. Celebi is movie four; movie one is Mewtwo.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`]},{id:`ani-m5`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Pokémon Heroes is Latias and Latios, 2002 Japan`,stat:`2002`,sub:`Japan July 13, 2002. US May 16, 2003.`,why:`Pokémon Heroes opened in Japan on July 13, 2002 and in the US on May 16, 2003. Alto Mare, Latias, and Latios. It is the last of the original-series theatricals.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`]},{id:`ani-m6`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Jirachi: Wish Maker is the first Advanced Generation film`,stat:`2003`,sub:`Japan July 19, 2003. US 2004, not a wide smash.`,why:`Jirachi: Wish Maker opened in Japan on July 19, 2003 with May and Max in the party. The US home and TV window was 2004. From here the West often skipped wide theatrical, while Japan still got a summer Toho date every year.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`]},{id:`ani-m7`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Destiny Deoxys is movie seven, 2004 Japan`,stat:`2004`,sub:`Japan July 17, 2004. US Cartoon Network 2005.`,why:`Destiny Deoxys opened in Japan on July 17, 2004. The US largely got it on Cartoon Network on January 22, 2005. A Cartoon Network premiere is not a 1999-style Warner Bros. wide release.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`]},{id:`ani-m8`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Lucario and the Mystery of Mew is movie eight`,stat:`2005`,sub:`Japan July 16, 2005. US September 19, 2006.`,why:`Lucario and the Mystery of Mew opened in Japan on July 16, 2005 and reached the US on September 19, 2006. It introduces Lucario to many English viewers before Diamond and Pearl. This Lucario is not Roy's 2025 Shiny.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`lucario`]},{id:`ani-m9`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Pokémon Ranger and the Temple of the Sea is movie nine`,stat:`2006`,sub:`Manaphy. Japan July 15, 2006. US March 23, 2007.`,why:`The last Advanced Generation film opened in Japan on July 15, 2006, with Manaphy and a Pokémon Ranger plot. The US window was March 23, 2007. May's last movie, then Diamond and Pearl films start the next Japan summer.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`]},{id:`ani-m10`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`The Rise of Darkrai opens the Sinnoh movie trio`,stat:`2007`,sub:`Japan July 14, 2007. US February 24, 2008.`,why:`The Rise of Darkrai opened in Japan on July 14, 2007 and in the US on February 24, 2008. Dialga, Palkia, Darkrai, Alamos Town. It is movie ten, the first Diamond and Pearl film, with Dawn in the party.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`]},{id:`ani-m11`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Giratina and the Sky Warrior is movie eleven`,stat:`2008`,sub:`Japan July 19, 2008. US February 13, 2009. Shaymin too.`,why:`Giratina and the Sky Warrior opened in Japan on July 19, 2008 and in the US on February 13, 2009. Giratina, Shaymin, Reverse World. Second of the Sinnoh legendary trio of films. Japan summer, US winter is the pattern here.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`]},{id:`ani-m12`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Arceus and the Jewel of Life is movie twelve`,stat:`2009`,sub:`Japan July 18, 2009. US TV later that year.`,why:`Arceus and the Jewel of Life opened in Japan on July 18, 2009. The US got a Cartoon Network window later in 2009. It closes the Darkrai–Giratina–Arceus movie run. Arceus on a poster is not Legends Arceus the 2022 game.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`]},{id:`ani-m13`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Zoroark: Master of Illusions is movie thirteen`,stat:`2010`,sub:`Japan July 10, 2010. US February 8, 2011.`,why:`Zoroark: Master of Illusions opened in Japan on July 10, 2010, still a Diamond and Pearl film with Dawn. The US Cartoon Network date was February 8, 2011. It is not a Black and White movie yet.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`]},{id:`ani-m14`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Victini and Zekrom, or Reshiram, is movie fourteen`,stat:`2011`,sub:`Two Japan cuts, July 16, 2011. US December 3.`,why:`Japan released White—Victini and Zekrom and Black—Victini and Reshiram on July 16, 2011, two cuts of movie fourteen. The US combined that into a December 3, 2011 window. First Best Wishes film, with Iris and Cilan, not Dawn.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`]},{id:`ani-m15`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Kyurem vs. the Sword of Justice is movie fifteen`,stat:`2012`,sub:`Japan July 14, 2012. US December 8, 2012. Keldeo.`,why:`Kyurem vs. the Sword of Justice opened in Japan on July 14, 2012 and in the US on December 8, 2012. Keldeo and the Swords of Justice. A December US date is still the previous Japan's summer movie.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`]},{id:`ani-m16`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Genesect and the Legend Awakened brings Mewtwo back`,stat:`2013`,sub:`Japan July 13, 2013. US October 19, 2013.`,why:`Extreme-Speed Genesect: Mewtwo Awakens opened in Japan on July 13, 2013 and in the US on October 19, 2013. A different Mewtwo than the 1998 clone story. Two Mewtwo movies already existed before the 2019 remake.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`mewtwo`]},{id:`ani-m17`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Diancie and the Cocoon of Destruction is the first XY film`,stat:`2014`,sub:`Japan July 19, 2014. US November 8, 2014. Serena is in it.`,why:`Diancie and the Cocoon of Destruction opened in Japan on July 19, 2014 and in the US on November 8, 2014. First Kalos theatrical, with Serena, Clemont, and Bonnie. It is movie seventeen, not a Sinnoh leftover.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`]},{id:`ani-m18`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Hoopa and the Clash of Ages is movie eighteen`,stat:`2015`,sub:`Japan July 18, 2015. US later that year on TV.`,why:`Hoopa and the Clash of Ages opened in Japan on July 18, 2015. The US window was late 2015. Hoopa Confined and Unbound, a pile of legendaries. A legendary cameo storm is still one Japan summer movie, not a new TV series.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`]},{id:`ani-m19`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Volcanion and the Mechanical Marvel closes the XY films`,stat:`2016`,sub:`Japan July 16, 2016. Magearna. Last Kalos theatrical.`,why:`Volcanion and the Mechanical Marvel opened in Japan on July 16, 2016, with Volcanion and Magearna. Last movie with the XY traveling party. The next Japan summer, I Choose You!, reboots Ash into another timeline.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`]},{id:`ani-m20`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`I Choose You is a 2017 alternate-timeline Ash movie`,stat:`2017`,sub:`Japan July 15. US Fathom Events November 5.`,why:`Pokémon the Movie: I Choose You! opened in Japan on July 15, 2017 and in US theaters via Fathom Events on November 5, 2017. It retells a Pallet Town start with a different party. It is not EP001 and not the weekly continuity.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`ash`,`indigo`]},{id:`ani-m21`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`The Power of Us is movie twenty-one, 2018`,stat:`2018`,sub:`Japan July 13. US November 24. Same alt timeline.`,why:`The Power of Us opened in Japan on July 13, 2018 and in the US on November 24, 2018. It continues the I Choose You! timeline, not the weekly Sun and Moon show. A theatrical Ash is not always the Friday-night Ash.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`ash`]},{id:`ani-m22`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Mewtwo Strikes Back Evolution is a 2019 CG remake`,stat:`2019`,sub:`Japan July 12, 2019. US Netflix February 27, 2020.`,why:`Mewtwo Strikes Back—Evolution remakes the 1998 film in CG. Japan theatrical July 12, 2019, US Netflix February 27, 2020. It is movie twenty-two in the animated count, not a new plot. Detective Pikachu that same year is live action and not this number.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`mewtwo`]},{id:`ani-m23`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Secrets of the Jungle is movie twenty-three, delayed to Christmas 2020`,stat:`2020`,sub:`Japan December 25, 2020. US Netflix October 8, 2021.`,why:`Secrets of the Jungle, titled Coco in Japan, was slated for July 10, 2020, then delayed by COVID-19 to December 25, 2020. US Netflix got it October 8, 2021. Last OLM theatrical in that annual run.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`]},{id:`ani-wild-card`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Wild Card is a 2027 Mimikyu movie from CloverWorks`,stat:`2027`,sub:`A TCG player, not Ash. First animated feature since 2020.`,why:`Pokémon: Wild Card was announced at the 2026 Pokémon World Championships for a 2027 release. CloverWorks animates, and Katsuhiko Kitada directs. The lead is a TCG player with a Mimikyu, not Ash and not Liko.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`wildcard`,`mimikyu`]},{id:`ani-wild-card-olm`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Wild Card is the first main animated film not made by OLM`,stat:`2027`,sub:`CloverWorks. Story Inc. Toho in Japan.`,why:`OLM made the TV series and the first twenty-three animated theatricals. Wild Card moves animation to CloverWorks, with Story Inc. producing and Toho distributing in Japan. It is still a Pokémon movie, not a TV episode of Horizons.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`wildcard`,`mimikyu`]},{id:`ani-detective`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Detective Pikachu is live action, not animated movie twenty-two`,stat:`2019`,sub:`May 10, 2019 US. Ryan Reynolds. Separate continuity.`,why:`POKÉMON Detective Pikachu opened in the US on May 10, 2019 as live action from Legendary Pictures. Japan lists it under other movies, not the numbered Pocket Monsters theatricals. Animated movie twenty-two that year is Mewtwo Strikes Back—Evolution.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`]},{id:`ani-annual`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Japan got a Pokémon theatrical almost every summer from 1998 to 2019`,stat:`22`,sub:`Then a 2020 delay. Then a gap until Wild Card.`,why:`Toho released an animated Pokémon film each Japanese summer from 1998 through 2019. Secrets of the Jungle slipped to December 25, 2020, then the annual movie slot stopped. Wild Card in 2027 is the next feature.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`]},{id:`ani-us-theatrical`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Only the first few films had a real US wide theatrical`,stat:`US`,sub:`Then TV, home video, Fathom, Netflix. Japan still had Toho.`,why:`Warner Bros. put the first movies in US cinemas. After that, most English releases were Cartoon Network, home video, limited Fathom Events, or Netflix. Japan kept summer theatricals.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`]},{id:`ani-fathom`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`I Choose You had a US Fathom Events night, not a 1999-style wide run`,stat:`2017`,sub:`November 5, 2017. Special theatrical, then home.`,why:`By 2017 the US did not treat Pokémon movies like the 1999 Warner Bros. wide release. Fathom Events booked I Choose You! for November 5, 2017. Japan had a normal July 15 Toho opening.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`ash`]},{id:`ani-m23-covid`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Secrets of the Jungle missed its July 10, 2020 Japan date`,stat:`COVID`,sub:`Moved to December 25, 2020. Netflix US the next October.`,why:`COVID-19 delayed the last OLM theatrical from July 10, 2020 to Christmas Day 2020 in Japan. English Netflix followed on October 8, 2021. That gap is a pandemic delay plus localization, not the old one-year theatrical lag from Mewtwo's 1998/1999 pair.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`]},{id:`ani-lucario-aura`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`The 2005 Lucario movie is where English TV met aura`,stat:`MS008`,sub:`Sir Aaron. Not Mega Lucario. Not Roy.`,why:`Lucario and the Mystery of Mew teaches aura through a Lucario sealed by Sir Aaron. Japan 2005, US 2006. Mega Lucario in Horizons is a 2025 battle mechanic on Roy's Shiny, same species, twenty years, two stories.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`lucario`]},{id:`ani-ms-codes`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Movie codes run MS001 through MS023, then Wild Card`,stat:`MS`,sub:`MS001 is Mewtwo 1998. MS023 is Jungle 2020.`,why:`Fans number the animated theatricals MS001–MS023. MS001 is Mewtwo Strikes Back, MS008 is Lucario, MS022 is Evolution, and MS023 is Secrets of the Jungle. Wild Card is the next animated feature, not a live-action Detective number.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`mewtwo`,`wildcard`]},{id:`ani-kitada`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Katsuhiko Kitada is directing Wild Card at CloverWorks`,stat:`2027`,sub:`Akemi Hayashi designs. Announced August 31, 2026.`,why:`The Wild Card teaser named Katsuhiko Kitada as director and Akemi Hayashi as character designer, animation by CloverWorks, announcement at Worlds 2026 on August 31. That staff list is the movie, not an OLM Friday episode credit.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`wildcard`,`mimikyu`]},{id:`ani-worlds-tease`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Wild Card was teased at the 2026 Pokémon World Championships`,stat:`AUG 31`,sub:`A 39-second teaser. Release is 2027, not that weekend.`,why:`The Pokémon Company showed a Wild Card teaser during the 2026 Worlds closing ceremony. A worlds trailer is marketing. The film is dated 2027 and beyond, so watching the teaser does not mean the movie is out.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`wildcard`,`mimikyu`]},{id:`ani-mimikyu-show`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Mimikyu's movie is Wild Card, not a Sun and Moon recap`,stat:`2027`,sub:`TCG player partner. Alola had Mimikyu too. Different story.`,why:`Sun and Moon used Mimikyu in the Alola weekly show and in Jessie's team. Wild Card's Mimikyu is a 2027 film partner for a card player. Same species, new continuity.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`wildcard`,`mimikyu`]},{id:`ani-mewtwo-origin`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`The Uncut Story of Mewtwo's Origin is a special, not movie one`,stat:`SPECIAL`,sub:`A prologue. The 1998 film is still the theatrical.`,why:`Japan released a Mewtwo origin special beside the first movie's world. English fans often saw it later on home video. It is backstory, not a replacement for the July 18, 1998 theatrical and not the 2019 CG remake.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`mewtwo`]},{id:`ani-mewtwo-returns`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Mewtwo Returns is a 2000 TV special, not a theatrical sequel number`,stat:`2000`,sub:`Japan TV. English home video later. Not movie two.`,why:`Mewtwo Returns is a television special after the first film, not The Power of One. Movie two is Lugia. Theatrical numbers stay on the summer Toho films.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`mewtwo`,`ash`]},{id:`ani-m22-not-live`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Evolution on Netflix is the CG Mewtwo remake, February 2020`,stat:`2020`,sub:`US stream. Japan had the remake in theaters in 2019.`,why:`English Netflix dated Mewtwo Strikes Back—Evolution to February 27, 2020. Japan had already shown it July 12, 2019. A Netflix movie tile next to Horizons is still the 1998 plot in new CG, not Detective Pikachu and not Wild Card.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`mewtwo`]},{id:`ani-olm`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`OLM has animated the weekly Pokémon show since 1997`,stat:`OLM`,sub:`TV Tokyo Friday. Wild Card's movie studio is the exception.`,why:`OLM, Inc. has produced the weekly series from Indigo through Horizons. The numbered theatricals through Secrets of the Jungle were OLM too. CloverWorks on Wild Card is the film break, not a recast of the Friday night TV show.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`indigo`]},{id:`ani-codes`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Episode codes change letters when Japan starts a new series`,stat:`CODES`,sub:`EP, AG, DP, BW, XY, SM, JN, HZ. Not US season numbers.`,why:`Fans number Japan episodes EP, AG, DP, BW, XY, SM, JN, then HZ. Those letters follow Japanese series, not English season 1–28. HZ150 is Horizons episode 150, not Ash season 28 episode 150.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`horizons`]},{id:`ani-season-count`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`English lists 25 Ash seasons, then Horizons as a new show`,stat:`25`,sub:`Japan counted eight series by Horizons, not 25 titles.`,why:`The West retroactively grouped Ash into 25 seasons across eight series names. Horizons is a second English animated series, with its own season 1. Do not treat Netflix season 3 of Horizons as Ash season 28.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`ash`,`horizons`]},{id:`ani-side-stories`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Pokémon Chronicles is a dub of Japanese side stories`,stat:`EXTRA`,sub:`Not the main numbering. Tracey, Gary, Team Rocket solo.`,why:`Japan aired Pocket Monsters Side Stories while the main show ran. English packaged many as Pokémon Chronicles. They are extras, not EP numbers in the main count.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`indigo`]},{id:`ani-winter`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Pikachu's Winter Vacation specials are not gym episodes`,stat:`SHORT`,sub:`Holiday shorts. Japan TV extras. Separate from EP codes.`,why:`Japan ran Pikachu holiday shorts and winter vacation specials beside the weekly plot. They are not Indigo League episode numbers and not movies. A Pikachu-only Christmas tape is still not EP001.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`indigo`]},{id:`ani-banned-beach`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Beauty and the Beach never aired in the original US run`,stat:`EP018`,sub:`4Kids skipped it. Later English releases vary.`,why:`EP018, Beauty and the Beach, with Team Rocket's inflatable gag, was pulled from the original 4Kids US broadcast. Japan had aired it. The Porygon episode is a different, medical ban.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`4kids`,`indigo`]},{id:`ani-tvtokyo`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Japan still airs the weekly show on TV Tokyo Fridays`,stat:`TX`,sub:`6:55 p.m. JST in the Horizons years. Not Netflix live.`,why:`The Japan broadcast is a TV Tokyo Friday slot, 18:55 JST in the current Horizons stretch. HZ150 is that slot on September 18, 2026. US Netflix is a later dubbed batch.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`horizons`]},{id:`ani-ikue-cap`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Ōtani voices both Ash's Pikachu and Captain Pikachu`,stat:`JP`,sub:`Same actress. Two characters. Do not merge them.`,why:`Ikue Ōtani kept voicing Pikachu after Ash left, now as Captain Pikachu in Horizons. That is continuity of a performer, not continuity of Ash's partner. Friede's Cap has a hat and a crew.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`captain`,`indigo`]},{id:`ani-count`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`The weekly series passed 1,300 Japan episodes`,stat:`1300+`,sub:`HZ numbers sit on top of EP through JN, not instead of them.`,why:`Japan's overall count was already past 1,200 when Horizons began and past 1,300 during Mega Voltage. HZ090 is also overall episode 1,322. The Japan overall count does not reset when the lead changes.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`horizons`]},{id:`ani-not-origins`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Pokémon Origins is a 2013 special, not the 1997 show`,stat:`2013`,sub:`Red and Green retold. Four parts. Separate from Ash.`,why:`Pokémon Origins retells the Red and Green games in four 2013 specials. It is not Indigo League and not a recap of EP001. A game-faithful special is not the 4Kids Saturday morning.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`indigo`]},{id:`ani-twilight`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Twilight Wings is a Galar web series, not Journeys`,stat:`2020`,sub:`Shorts on YouTube. John, not Ash, not Liko.`,why:`Pokémon: Twilight Wings is a 2020 Galar mini-series of shorts, released in Japan and English close together on YouTube. It is not Pokémon Journeys and not a movie. Keep web shorts off the HZ and JN code list.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`]},{id:`ani-generations`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Pokémon Generations is a 2016 game recap, not XY`,stat:`2016`,sub:`Eighteen shorts. English first on YouTube.`,why:`Pokémon Generations retells game plots in 18 shorts. English YouTube began September 16, 2016, before the Japan drop. It is not the weekly XY series and not a film.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`]},{id:`ani-concierge`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Pokémon Concierge is stop-motion on Netflix, not Horizons`,stat:`2023`,sub:`Haru at a hotel. Separate from Liko's Friday night.`,why:`Pokémon Concierge is a stop-motion Netflix series from Dwarf Studios, 2023, about a hotel concierge named Haru. It is not Horizons, not Ash, and not a TCG product. Netflix carrying both does not make them the same show.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`]},{id:`ani-tpci`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Later English seasons are TPCi dubs, not 4Kids`,stat:`TPCI`,sub:`Closer to Japan names and plots after the studio split.`,why:`The Pokémon Company International handled later English versions. Renamed food and missing Team Rocket motto verses are 4Kids-era tells. Horizons on Netflix is a TPCi dub, not a 4Kids show.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`horizons`,`4kids`]},{id:`ani-evolving-again`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Evolving Again is the English Rising Hope opening`,stat:`OP`,sub:`Haven Paschall vocals. Japan used GET BACK on Mega Voltage.`,why:`English Rising Hope uses Evolving Again, written with Ed Goldfarb, sung by Haven Paschall. Japan opened Mega Voltage with GET BACK. A Netflix theme is not proof Japan heard that song on April 11, 2025.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`horizons`]},{id:`ani-rising-again`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Japan's Rising Again arc is not the English season title`,stat:`JP`,sub:`Arc names in Japan. Rising Hope is the English season.`,why:`Japan splits Horizons into story arcs with their own logos: Mega Voltage, Rising Again, Wonder Voyage. English Netflix sells season 3 as Rising Hope in parts. HZ150 sits in Wonder Voyage, not in a Netflix part title.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`horizons`]},{id:`ani-asia-yt`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Pokémon Asia ENG YouTube is another English Horizons clock`,stat:`SEA`,sub:`Not Netflix US. Season 3 went up there in March 2026.`,why:`Southeast Asia's Pokémon Asia ENG channel posted Horizons English batches on a different day than US Netflix. Rising Hope's later parts showed that split again in 2026. If a friend in Singapore already saw an episode, your US Netflix part may still be unlisted.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`horizons`]},{id:`ani-friede-goggles`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Friede's goggles on a Charmander are a GO crossover look`,stat:`GO`,sub:`September 16–22, 2026 in GO. TV episode is HZ150.`,why:`Pokémon GO's Horizons event, September 16–22, 2026, put Friede's goggles on Charmander and offered Captain Pikachu. HZ150 on September 18 is the TV crossover. Phone skins are not the episode.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`horizons`,`captain`]},{id:`ani-horizons-tera`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Horizons uses Terastal because it is the Paldea show`,stat:`TERA`,sub:`Crystal hats. Not Dynamax. Not Z-Moves.`,why:`Liko and Roy Terastallize in Horizons the way Scarlet and Violet do. Journeys used Dynamax in Galar, Sun and Moon used Z-Moves, and XY used Mega. Rising Hope's 2026 copy lists Tera and Mega together because Horizons later has both.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`horizons`,`lucario`]},{id:`ani-no-dex-standin`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`A Pokédex Pikachu is not a screenshot of the cartoon`,stat:`STILL`,sub:`Show stills for TV facts. Dex art is the games.`,why:`The weekly show is OLM animation on TV Tokyo, dubbed later for Netflix and iPlayer. Official game artwork of Pikachu is not Ash's partner on Friday night and not Captain Pikachu on the Brave Olivine. Use the show still; the Dex model is a different product.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`indigo`,`horizons`,`captain`]},{id:`ani-ann`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Ann is Liko's school friend, not a second protagonist`,stat:`HZ`,sub:`Indigo Academy later. A classmate, not Roy.`,why:`Ann appears as Liko's friend around school, then again when Liko is at Indigo Academy in Mega Voltage. She is support cast. Roy is the other lead.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`horizons`,`liko`]},{id:`ani-brock-food`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Brock is the traveling cook, then a Pokémon doctor`,stat:`OS`,sub:`Pewter gym first. Hospital later. Not Friede's job.`,why:`Brock feeds the party through Kanto, Johto, Hoenn, and Sinnoh, then leaves to study as a Pokémon doctor. Friede is a professor-captain on an airship with a different cook, Murdock. If someone is flirting and frying eggs in 2008, that is Brock.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`ash`,`indigo`]},{id:`ani-pikachu-thunder`,kicker:`Anime`,headline:`Ash's Pikachu learns Thunderbolt early and keeps it iconic`,stat:`OS`,sub:`A character move. Game Pikachu can learn other sets.`,why:`The TV Pikachu's Thunderbolt is a signature, not a competitive set, and Volt Tackle comes later as a character upgrade. Captain Pikachu has his own battle style under Friede. Do not teach a game Pikachu from a still of either show.`,tags:[`anime`,`fact`,`ash`,`indigo`,`captain`]}],i={note:e,asOf:t,count:204,facts:r};export{t as asOf,n as count,i as default,r as facts,e as note};